    "events": [
      {
        "met_days": 0,
        "type": "launch",
        "label": "Launch — Falcon Heavy",
        "description": "Kennedy Space Center LC-39A, 2023-10-13. Psyche is NASA's first mission to a metal-rich M-class asteroid — a 226-km-diameter body believed to be the exposed nickel-iron core of a differentiated proto-planet. Insight into Psyche's interior is insight into the cores of every rocky planet, including Earth's."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 0.005,
        "type": "tli_or_tmi",
        "label": "Earth departure",
        "description": "Falcon Heavy second stage delivers Psyche to escape velocity. Ion thrusters take over within hours of separation — continuous low-thrust acceleration shapes the entire transfer."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 30,
        "type": "tli_or_tmi",
        "label": "Ion-propulsion cruise begins",
        "description": "Hall-effect ion thrusters (four SPT-140s, 4.5 kW each) commissioned. Continuous low-thrust acceleration over the next 4+ years gradually expands the heliocentric orbit toward Psyche."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 945,
        "type": "flyby",
        "label": "Mars gravity assist",
        "description": "2026-05-15. Closest approach 3,000 km. Adds the orbital energy that ion thrust alone couldn't deliver in a reasonable time frame, accelerating Psyche outward to the main belt."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 1500,
        "type": "tcm",
        "label": "Cruise refinement",
        "description": "Mid-cruise trajectory refinement. Ion thrust continues."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2118,
        "type": "arrival",
        "label": "Psyche approach",
        "description": "Planned 2029-08. Psyche enters the asteroid's gravitational sphere of influence. Ion engines slow the spacecraft for gradual capture."
      },
      {
        "met_days": 2118.005,
        "type": "edl_or_oi",
        "label": "Psyche orbit insertion",
        "description": "Ion-thrust gravitational capture into a high orbit (700 km altitude). Over the following 26-month mission, Psyche will descend through four orbit phases (A → B → C → D), mapping the asteroid's gravity, magnetism, topography, and composition. If the metal-core hypothesis is right, Psyche will look fundamentally unlike any other small body we've seen — exposed metal surface with iron-sulphide deposits, none of the rubble-pile chaos of typical asteroids."
      }
    ],
